"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[22200],{322200:e=>{e.exports="# Oracle Error Handling → Databricks SQL\n\nOracle `EXCEPTION WHEN ... THEN` blocks → DBSQL `DECLARE ... HANDLER`. Covers predefined exceptions, user-defined named exceptions, `PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT`, propagation across nested blocks, and the `DBMS_UTILITY.FORMAT_*` / `UTL_CALL_STACK.*` introspection helpers. Split out of `04-procedures-variables.md` so the exception-mapping tables load on demand.\n\n> Companion files:\n>\n> - `04-procedures-variables.md` — surrounding procedure / function syntax, transaction control, `PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION`, `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` exception capture.\n> - `04a-cursors.md` — the `DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND` pattern in cursor loops uses the handler syntax described in this file.\n> - `00-quirks.md` \xa7TODO Reference — `<name> EXCEPTION;` declarations and predefined-exception catalog drop rules.\n\n## ORACLE\n\n### Error Handling (EXCEPTION → DECLARE HANDLER)\n\nOracle uses named `EXCEPTION` blocks. DBSQL uses `DECLARE ... HANDLER` inside a `BEGIN ... END` block. Handlers must be declared **before** any executable statements in the block.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No data');\n    WHEN OTHERS THEN\n        RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001, 'Insert failed: ' || SQLERRM);\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n    BEGIN\n        -- SQLSTATE and MESSAGE_TEXT are available in handler scope\n        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Insert failed';\n    END;\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Exception mapping:**\n\n| Oracle                                   | DBSQL                                                                 | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ---------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `BEGIN ... EXCEPTION WHEN ... END;`      | `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION BEGIN ... END` at top of block | Handler declared at top of block; `EXIT` means exit the block on error                                                                                                                      |\n| `RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001, 'msg')` | `SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'msg'`                    | SQLSTATE `'45000'` is the generic unhandled exception state. Oracle's error code `-20001` has no direct DBSQL representation.                                                               |\n| `RAISE;` (re-raise)                      | `RESIGNAL`                                                            | Re-raises the current exception inside a handler                                                                                                                                            |\n| `RAISE my_named_exception`               | `SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = '...'`                    | User-defined named exceptions have no DBSQL equivalent — use a SIGNAL with a descriptive message and document the original exception name in a comment                                      |\n| `SQLERRM`                                | `MESSAGE_TEXT` condition information item                             | `GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 msg = MESSAGE_TEXT;`                                                                                                                                           |\n| `SQLCODE`                                | No direct equivalent — use `RETURNED_SQLSTATE`                        | `GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 s = RETURNED_SQLSTATE;` returns 5-char SQLSTATE string (e.g. `'22012'`). Oracle's numeric `SQLCODE` has no numeric DBSQL equivalent.                           |\n| `WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND`                     | `DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND ...`                               | `NOT FOUND` covers the no-rows case for cursor FETCH and `SELECT INTO`                                                                                                                      |\n| `WHEN DUP_VAL_ON_INDEX`                  | `DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '23505' ...`                        | Unique constraint violation. Note DBSQL enforces unique constraints only via schema checks; most duplicate-key issues manifest in `MERGE`.                                                  |\n| `WHEN ZERO_DIVIDE`                       | `DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '22012' ...`                        |                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `WHEN VALUE_ERROR`                       | `DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '22000' ...`                        | Generic data exception class                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `WHEN OTHERS`                            | `DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION ...`                            | Catches any unhandled exception. Must be the **last** WHEN branch in Oracle; multiple handlers in DBSQL collapse to one — discriminate on `RETURNED_SQLSTATE` inside the handler if needed. |\n\n**Predefined-exception catalog (long tail).** Oracle's `STANDARD` package exposes ~22 named exceptions. The most common six are above. The rest map to SQLSTATE classes or have no DBSQL counterpart at all:\n\n| Oracle predefined exception       | DBSQL handler / SQLSTATE                         | Notes                                                                                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `INVALID_NUMBER`                  | `HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '22018'`                   | String → number conversion failure in a SQL statement. Prefer `TRY_CAST(... AS BIGINT/DECIMAL)` and check for NULL — same outcome, no handler. |\n| `VALUE_ERROR`                     | `HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '22000'`                   | PL/SQL conversion / arithmetic error (the procedural counterpart of `INVALID_NUMBER`). Same `TRY_CAST` strategy applies.                       |\n| `CASE_NOT_FOUND`                  | `HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '20000'`                   | Raised by a `CASE` statement (not expression) with no matching `WHEN` and no `ELSE`. Add an explicit `ELSE` branch in the rewrite.             |\n| `CURSOR_ALREADY_OPEN`             | `HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '24000'`                   | Re-opening an open explicit cursor. In DBSQL the literal cursor pattern is rare — see `04a-cursors.md`.                                        |\n| `INVALID_CURSOR`                  | `HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '24000'`                   | Operating on an unopened/closed cursor. Same as above.                                                                                         |\n| `ROWTYPE_MISMATCH`                | `HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '07006'`                   | Host cursor variable's `%ROWTYPE` doesn't match the assigned `REF CURSOR` shape. Resolve to concrete `STRUCT` types — no `%ROWTYPE` in DBSQL.  |\n| `SUBSCRIPT_BEYOND_COUNT`          | TODO — collection over-index                     | DBSQL has no PL/SQL collections. If translating a collection loop, bounds-check the array literal length explicitly.                           |\n| `SUBSCRIPT_OUTSIDE_LIMIT`         | TODO — negative or out-of-range collection index | Same — refactor to set-based SQL.                                                                                                              |\n| `COLLECTION_IS_NULL`              | TODO — uninitialized collection                  | Same — refactor to set-based SQL.                                                                                                              |\n| `ACCESS_INTO_NULL`                | No equivalent — drop                             | Object-type field assignment; not migratable.                                                                                                  |\n| `SELF_IS_NULL`                    | No equivalent — drop                             | Object-type member call on a null instance; not migratable.                                                                                    |\n| `PROGRAM_ERROR` / `STORAGE_ERROR` | `HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION` — emit TODO comment   | Internal Oracle conditions. Replace with the catch-all and document that the original was an internal-state failure.                           |\n| `SYS_INVALID_ROWID`               | No equivalent — drop the handler                 | DBSQL has no `ROWID`. The condition cannot occur after migration.                                                                              |\n| `TIMEOUT_ON_RESOURCE`             | No equivalent — drop the handler                 | Lock timeouts (`FOR UPDATE WAIT n`) — see `02-select-dml.md → FOR UPDATE`.                                                                     |\n| `NO_DATA_NEEDED`                  | No equivalent — drop the handler                 | Pipelined-table-function pruning signal. Pipelined functions don't migrate — see `04-procedures-variables.md` \xa7Table-valued functions.         |\n| `LOGIN_DENIED` / `NOT_LOGGED_ON`  | No equivalent — drop the handler                 | Session-level conditions; Databricks compute / Unity Catalog handle authentication differently.                                                |\n\nIf the source `WHEN <exception>` matches a row in this table, use the suggested SQLSTATE handler. If it matches a \"no equivalent\" row, drop the branch and emit a TODO comment naming the original exception.\n\n**`PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT` — naming a numeric error.** Oracle lets you bind a user-defined exception name to a specific ORA error code so that `WHEN <name>` catches that specific number:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    deadlock_detected EXCEPTION;\n    PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT(deadlock_detected, -60);\nBEGIN\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN deadlock_detected THEN\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Deadlock — retrying');\nEND;\n/\n```\n\nDBSQL has no `EXCEPTION_INIT` and no numeric error codes. Translate by **dropping the pragma and the exception declaration**, then rewriting the handler in one of two ways:\n\n- If the exception was being caught to swallow / log a known DB error, fall back to a `HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION` block — DBSQL does not surface Oracle's ORA-XXXXX numeric vocabulary.\n- If the body inspects the error before deciding what to do, capture `RETURNED_SQLSTATE` via `GET DIAGNOSTICS` and branch on the SQLSTATE class — but most `EXCEPTION_INIT`-bound codes are Oracle-internal (lock waits, deadlocks, redo conditions) and have no SQLSTATE counterpart at all.\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL — drop the pragma; keep behavior as a generic catch\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n    BEGIN\n        -- Oracle source caught ORA-00060 (deadlock_detected) here.\n        -- Databricks does not surface lock-deadlock conditions to user code.\n        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Update failed';\n    END;\n    UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nEND;\n```\n\n**User-defined exceptions (`<name> EXCEPTION;`).** Oracle lets you declare a name in the DECLARE section and `RAISE` it later as a typed sentinel:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    insufficient_funds EXCEPTION;\nBEGIN\n    IF balance < amount THEN\n        RAISE insufficient_funds;\n    END IF;\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN insufficient_funds THEN\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Not enough money');\nEND;\n/\n```\n\nDBSQL has no named exception types. Translate as:\n\n1. **Drop** the `<name> EXCEPTION;` declaration entirely.\n2. **`RAISE <name>;`** → `SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = '<name> triggered: <description>'` — embed the original exception name in the message so downstream callers / logs can still recognize it.\n3. **`WHEN <name> THEN`** → if the handler is colocated with the `RAISE` (same procedure / block), inline the handler body into the conditional that previously raised. If the `RAISE` and `WHEN` are in different blocks, fall back to a `HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION` and detect by message text — but flag with a TODO since text-matching is fragile.\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL — preferred: refactor away from RAISE entirely\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE balance, amount DECIMAL(18, 2);\n    -- ... populate balance and amount ...\n    IF balance < amount THEN\n        -- Oracle source raised insufficient_funds here.\n        SELECT 'Not enough money';\n        -- or, if the caller expects an error:\n        -- SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'insufficient_funds: balance < amount';\n    END IF;\nEND;\n```\n\n**Exception propagation across nested blocks.** Oracle's exception model propagates an unhandled exception **up the block stack** until a matching `WHEN` is found or the outermost block re-raises to the caller. DBSQL handlers have the same enclosing-block-first scope: a handler declared in the inner block catches first; if absent, the exception propagates to the enclosing block. Practical implications when migrating:\n\n- A nested `BEGIN ... EXCEPTION ... END;` in Oracle that catches `OTHERS` and continues → translate to a nested DBSQL `BEGIN ... DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION ... END;`. The outer block continues running normally.\n- Oracle's `RAISE;` (no argument, re-raise current exception) inside a `WHEN` branch → DBSQL `RESIGNAL`. Both push the exception to the enclosing block.\n- An unhandled exception at the top of a stored procedure propagates to the caller in both dialects. No drift.\n\n**Error-stack helpers (`SQLERRM(n)`, `DBMS_UTILITY.FORMAT_*`).** Several Oracle introspection helpers have **no DBSQL equivalent**:\n\n| Oracle                                                | DBSQL                                             | Notes                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `SQLERRM(p_errcode)` (with arg)                       | No equivalent                                     | Returns the Oracle error message for a specific ORA code. Drop — DBSQL has no numeric error vocabulary to look up. |\n| `DBMS_UTILITY.FORMAT_ERROR_STACK`                     | `GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 msg = MESSAGE_TEXT;` | Closest available — single-line message, not a stack.                                                              |\n| `DBMS_UTILITY.FORMAT_CALL_STACK`                      | No equivalent — drop                              | DBSQL does not expose a procedural call stack to user code.                                                        |\n| `DBMS_UTILITY.FORMAT_ERROR_BACKTRACE`                 | No equivalent — drop                              | Same — no line-by-line backtrace.                                                                                  |\n| `UTL_CALL_STACK.*` (Oracle 12c+ structured backtrace) | No equivalent — drop                              | Same.                                                                                                              |\n\nIf the source uses these helpers to write to a log table, replace with `MESSAGE_TEXT` only and accept the loss of stack detail.\n\n**CONTINUE vs EXIT handlers:**\n\n- `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER` — exits the block when triggered (equivalent to Oracle `EXCEPTION` which exits the current block)\n- `DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER` — resumes execution after the failing statement (no direct Oracle equivalent; closest is catching the exception and continuing explicitly with `NULL;`)\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL: EXIT handler (most common Oracle equivalent)\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Transaction failed';\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL: CONTINUE handler (suppress errors and keep going)\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n    -- Used in cursor loops — see 04a-cursors.md\nEND;\n```\n"}}]);
